 # English Version: 
This is the "Malware Analysis" course
Dynamic Analysis of WinlockerVB6Blacksod Malware on Linux

Best Work

Objective
To help students master techniques for dynamic malware analysis, especially Winlocker-type malware, on the Linux operating system. Dynamically analyze a simple ransomware sample to identify unusual behavior.

Requirements for Students
Monitor processes and I/O behavior.

Analyze network traffic, monitor whether the ransomware sample connects to the C2 server.

Monitor encrypted files and file format changes.

Practical Content
Starting the lab:

Open the terminal, type:

labtainer mal-dmic-lin-ransom-lock

After starting, a virtual terminal will appear.

The lab has already created a .exe file, the WinlockerVB6Blacksod.exe ransomware sample.

Students check the files in the lab using the command:

Ls

Check the machine's IP address and network interface using the command:

Ifconfig

Task 1: Capture data via interface

Capture data via the eth0 interface and write it to the pcap file using the command:

Sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -w ret.pcap

All captured data will be saved to the ret.pcap file.

Task 2: Use Wine software

Open another lab window to run Wine.

Reconfigure Wine, select the Windows 8 framework for the lab using the command:

Winecfg

The Winecfg window will appear; students proceed to configure Windows 8 for the lab.

Run the Wine program for the sample file WinlockerVB6Blachsod.exe using the command:

Wine WinlockerVB6Blachsod.exe

The Wine window will appear. During the process, the system will prompt you to reconfigure Wine. Continue running until Wine stops and you will see the captured packets obtained from capturing data via the eth0 interface in the first labtainer window.

Observe the captured packets using the command:

Tcpdump -r ret.pcap

The captured packets will be saved to the ret.pcap file. Analyze the captured packets.

Task 3: Filter IP addresses communicating with the victim machine

Filter the IP addresses communicating with the victim machine using the command:

Tcpdump -r ret.pcap | grep -Eo ‘([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}’ | sort | uniq

Task 4: Monitor processes during sample execution

Monitor the processes during sample execution and write to the retProc.txt file using the command:

Strace -f -e strace=all wine WinlockerVB6Blacksod.exe > retProc.txt

Task 5: Monitor process creation

Monitor process creation via PID using the command:

Strace -f -c wine WinlockerVB6Blacksod.exe > retProc.txt

Task 6: Monitor file manipulation

Monitor file manipulation while the WinlockerVB6Blacksod.exe sample is running on Wine.

Strace -e strace=file wine WinlockerVB6Blacksod.exe

Checkwork:

Checkwork

In the terminal, first use the following command to end the lab:

stoplab

When the lab ends, a zip file containing the results will be created and saved to the location shown below. stoplab.

During the assignment, students need to repeat the lab using the command:

Labtainer -r mal-dmic-lin-ransom-lock


